{
 "name": "Ulster Scots",
 "endonym": "Ulstèr-Scotch",
 "about": [
  "Ulster Scots came across the North Channel with Scottish settlers from the early 1600s, and it's a living form of Lowland Scots.",
  "It's strongest in County Antrim, County Down (the Ards above all) and parts of County Londonderry and east Donegal.",
  "The 1998 Belfast/Good Friday Agreement named Ulster-Scots, with Irish, as part of the cultural wealth of the island of Ireland.",
  "Watch out: in Ulster Scots 'aye' means always. Yes is 'ay'."
 ],
 "places": {},
 "greetings": [
  "Bout ye!",
  "Fair faa ye!"
 ],
 "phrases": {
  "how_are_you": "Bout ye",
  "welcome": "Fair faa ye",
  "yes": "Ay",
  "small": "Wee",
  "ulster_scots": "Ulstèr-Scotch"
 },
 "lessons": [
  {
   "id": "sco_ulster_01",
   "title": "Ay, na and thon",
   "level": 1,
   "teacher": {
    "zone": "belfast_linenhall",
    "x": 10,
    "y": 3,
    "name": "Wilma",
    "sprite": "villager_2"
   },
   "intro": [
    "Half of Belfast talks a bit of Ulster Scots and doesn't know it.",
    "Wee, thon, the morra: you've heard them already. Let's make it official."
   ],
   "words": [
    {
     "id": "ay",
     "local": "Ay",
     "english": "Yes",
     "say": "EYE",
     "note": "'Aye' with an e means always: 'aye an on'."
    },
    {
     "id": "na",
     "local": "Na",
     "english": "No",
     "say": "NAH"
    },
    {
     "id": "wee",
     "local": "Wee",
     "english": "Small",
     "say": "WEE"
    },
    {
     "id": "thon",
     "local": "Thon",
     "english": "That (over there)",
     "say": "THON",
     "note": "Soft 'th', as in 'this'."
    },
    {
     "id": "thonder",
     "local": "Thonder",
     "english": "Yonder, over there",
     "say": "THON-der"
    },
    {
     "id": "wi",
     "local": "Wi",
     "english": "With",
     "say": "WIH"
    },
    {
     "id": "the_day",
     "local": "The day",
     "english": "Today",
     "say": "the DAY"
    },
    {
     "id": "the_morra",
     "local": "The morra",
     "english": "Tomorrow",
     "say": "the MOR-ra"
    },
    {
     "id": "the_nicht",
     "local": "The nicht",
     "english": "Tonight",
     "say": "the NIKHT",
     "note": "Throaty 'ch', like 'loch'. A sure sign of Ulster Scots."
    },
    {
     "id": "yin",
     "local": "Yin",
     "english": "One",
     "say": "YIN",
     "note": "Two is 'twa'. In Ulster English one is 'wan'."
    }
   ],
   "tip": "Many of these, like wee, thon and dander, are used by nearly everyone in Ulster. Words like 'nicht' and 'cannae' mark proper Ulster Scots."
  },
  {
